#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096326 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/id #uuid "74ffb6be-e709-5635-b6e2-98911c40a19e" :question/math-academy-id "e-184" :question/problem "Given that $y =\\dfrac{e^{3x}}{2^{x}+1}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=e^{3x}$ and the denominator\n\n$$\nv(x)=2^x +1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x)$, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2^x \\ln 2\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u$.\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ &=\\dfrac{3e^{3x} \\cdot \\left(2^{x}+1\\right) - e^{3x} \\cdot \\left(2^x \\cdot \\ln2\\right)}{\\left(2^{x}+1 \\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\dfrac{3e^{3x}2^{x} + 3e^{3x} - e^{3x}2^{x}\\ln 2}{\\left(2^{x}+1 \\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\dfrac{e^{3x} \\left(3(2^{x}) - 2^{x} \\ln 2 +3\\right)}{\\left(2^{x}+1 \\right)^2} \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096327 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/id #uuid "a2bb6a33-8a57-5b6b-9d35-cf611fbd5c9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-185" :question/problem "If $f(x) = e^{-2x}\\tan({x^2})$, then what is $f'(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let\n\n$$\nu(x) = e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\tan{(x^2)}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\nu'(x) = -2e^{-2x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2x\\sec^2(x^2)\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to calculate both $u'(x)$ and $v'(x)$.\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= u^{′}(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v^{′}(x) \\\\\n&= (-2e^{-2x}) \\cdot \\tan (x^{2}) + e^{-2x} \\cdot 2x\\sec^{2} (x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2e^{-2x}(-\\tan (x^{2}) + x\\sec^{2} (x^{2})) \\\\\n&= 2e^{-2x}(x\\sec^{2} (x^{2}) - \\tan (x^{2}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "b00f1213-cdaf-512c-9842-92cbee1ec63a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6100" :question/problem "Differentiate $y = \\cos^4{2x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dv} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dx} \\\\\n&= 4u^{3} \\cdot (-\\sin v) \\cdot (2) \\\\\n&= 4(\\cos 2x)^{3} \\cdot (-\\sin 2x) \\cdot (2) \\\\\n&=-8\\cos^{3} 2x\\sin 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e379f4ed-96fc-54e8-8343-e7cc7dbd548b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250071} {:answer/id #uuid "2f09a3ed-5703-5d97-89c0-061a8ca7796a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250072} {:answer/id #uuid "d67420c2-d9f9-5af0-8fa1-6c7483fbc14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250073} {:answer/id #uuid "e8067465-e26d-5981-81be-8eb81be4480d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250074} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f0ffea-2d18-5020-a1b2-92d74de3da0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8067465-e26d-5981-81be-8eb81be4480d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250074} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fd66f9f-c00b-54af-af6e-42d63823f63d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250070}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af401821-15de-511b-ae7d-83263ee65d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-10009" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=\\pi /2} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3aa463-cd3f-5a68-8b33-e28f2c51b2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x{e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250078} {:answer/id #uuid "39574bcf-9d44-56f9-89f4-8cb38b5f0839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x+10){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250079} {:answer/id #uuid "30de3564-36c3-5b89-a7d1-0ac882a7a75e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x+1){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250080} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cc61d7-1d4e-550e-abaf-a727fc21ef7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250081} {:answer/id #uuid "daf469be-b289-52dd-8823-1062d5390f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(x+1){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39574bcf-9d44-56f9-89f4-8cb38b5f0839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x+10){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250079} :answer-field/id #uuid "b81c9e8f-3785-5c2e-a976-836a987cad2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250077}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac4f2143-0b5a-5a4d-94fb-d6c2015bc5dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-11824" :question/problem "Given that $y=(x+1){e}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+1$ and $v(x)={e}^{9x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=9{e}^{9x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(1)({e}^{9x})+(x+1)(9{e}^{9x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{9x}+9(x+1){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(1+9(x+1)){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(9x+10){e}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dcf0751-f342-57ee-88f5-7ab04150fb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6x)/((3x + 5)^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094083} {:answer/id #uuid "be64dd14-3ea1-544b-93f1-d27c028793ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x)/((3x + 5)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094084} {:answer/id #uuid "c47e1a10-0bcb-5123-8b30-228dd01ee56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5x)/((3x + 5)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094085} {:answer/id #uuid "10ec327f-9b94-56da-9c7c-e97d2c86f0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(30x)/((3x + 5)^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094086} {:answer/id #uuid "4df11836-1664-5e47-ba48-93d52815bd1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x)/((3x + 5)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ec327f-9b94-56da-9c7c-e97d2c86f0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(30x)/((3x + 5)^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094086} :answer-field/id #uuid "a17443b5-e03a-52af-a8f8-80174508b99f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094082}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4603accf-ccdb-5030-90b8-c9b568c4df0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11973" :question/problem "Given that $y = (3x^{2})/((3x + 5)^{2})$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$." :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)=(3x+5)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=6x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=6(3x+5).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6x){(3x+5)}^{2}-(3{x}^{2})\\cdot 6(3x+5)}{{((3x+5)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6x(3x+5)(3x+5-3x)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6x(3x+5)(5)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30x}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9edca068-3380-5f7b-8546-48890d080555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250085} {:answer/id #uuid "350a83e0-7923-5b08-9392-87ad87b493ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250086} {:answer/id #uuid "0d475a46-ac9e-5a93-a5f8-a773369ddfa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(x+1)}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250087} {:answer/id #uuid "447e4b73-1241-5aa4-b059-4f8b11f3010d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-x)}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250088} {:answer/id #uuid "80b1e053-447b-51ae-ad6d-1678ce0ad780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1-x)^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "350a83e0-7923-5b08-9392-87ad87b493ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250086} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69f7ba6-4320-560f-9024-6f57f32bc283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92523772-6de5-5832-955f-4e96488833e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-11978" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=(x+1)^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{2}+1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2(x+1),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(x+1))({x}^{2}+1)-((x+1)^{2})(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)-2x(x+1)^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(({x}^{2}+1)-x(x+1))}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1-{x}^{2}-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(1-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e72bb9e-bf50-5f05-8fea-6643e8e5c873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250092} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca4322b-0aa8-589a-b696-325d8ce56f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250093} {:answer/id #uuid "71930411-4b10-53ef-95f7-2491fbb7401b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250094} {:answer/id #uuid "875759ff-ea48-5385-ad0e-3d605905ea15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sec}^{2}⁡x+3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250095} {:answer/id #uuid "ae968b51-4ba0-5eb1-ac0c-c3267ee5d579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae968b51-4ba0-5eb1-ac0c-c3267ee5d579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250096} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bb343fc-e533-5bb4-8af7-1e974f8437a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250091}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cc0b95c-1856-5de2-836b-234ed6403eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-2392" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={a}^{3x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $v(x)={a}^{3x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡a.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡x)({a}^{3x})+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡a) \\\\  & ={a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "980aecfb-183b-5fea-a534-a74eef4c3965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7\\sec^{2} (7x - 2) - \\tan (7x - 2))/(16x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095077} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b54e6c-32bd-5c6a-9dbd-a211d0225b34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7x\\sec^{2} (7x - 2) - \\tan (7x - 2))/(16x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095078} {:answer/id #uuid "e768b7a0-f83f-5d0b-80dd-898cdf867862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sec^{2} (7x - 2) - \\tan (7x - 2))/(4x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095079} {:answer/id #uuid "3bda0a1f-ba43-5bc2-b4c6-f603bdb9d760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7x\\sec^{2} (7x - 2) - \\tan (7x - 2))/(4x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095080} {:answer/id #uuid "3f828d78-a786-5edd-b326-6e3442a41b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x\\sec^{2} (7x - 2) + \\tan (7x - 2))/(4x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bda0a1f-ba43-5bc2-b4c6-f603bdb9d760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7x\\sec^{2} (7x - 2) - \\tan (7x - 2))/(4x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095080} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8fcf880-e9ed-53c6-b887-3e0063f1c7bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095076}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cae07ba-0147-5b66-85db-519e500d15f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-2396" :question/problem "Find $y^{′}$ for $y = (\\tan (7x - 2))/(4x)$." :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)$ and the denominator $v(x)=4x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=7{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2))(4x)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2))}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{⧸}4(7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2))}{\\text{⧸}4(4{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee8b686-f04d-59d9-814b-0cff2488cc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250099} {:answer/id #uuid "0443124a-8281-51d1-b894-2ffd12c7720c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250100} {:answer/id #uuid "a3983a1f-39c0-5fd8-a655-fc9c26a7b2d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54x{sin}^{3}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{3}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250101} {:answer/id #uuid "a7aabba9-c859-551c-b1c1-66bb18472e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250102} {:answer/id #uuid "526e71e6-bf02-5b8d-8dad-6e915d6faad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "526e71e6-bf02-5b8d-8dad-6e915d6faad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250103} :answer-field/id #uuid "022daecd-d7b4-5767-9457-1f7dac3ee10b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250098}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a7ed7da-3b96-5321-aaa3-c57d048a0cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-2397" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={sin}^{3}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v=9{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=18x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)\\cdot 18x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1))^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1))\\cdot 18x \\\\  & =54x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d2cb22f-759e-5f66-a11b-d07d0cabc3ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250106} {:answer/id #uuid "a1470e22-19d9-5b99-b4aa-1e2119168a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250107} {:answer/id #uuid "d9123221-05a5-5ca8-a2a4-3b151d06032a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250108} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7afe86-015e-5f44-b80a-651c68887da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250109} {:answer/id #uuid "52a505e2-c0a0-5da5-a805-b4d632490090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9123221-05a5-5ca8-a2a4-3b151d06032a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250108} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4da8a68-57fa-5444-88d2-e51880ea8d0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250105}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19119cab-b147-5de2-999a-940899f58d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-28655" :question/problem "Find ${h}^{′}(0)$ when $h(x)={5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $h$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nh={5}^{u},\\,u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,v=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\frac{1}{v},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{v})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${h}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0280610-74d3-5e76-afb5-570b31cd22c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x+6)\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250113} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce87378-dd6f-5d41-9954-63bd96b1d88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x+6){csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250114} {:answer/id #uuid "67f01430-60c9-577f-863c-f493f42cd459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250115} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf6d281-db07-5a56-b15f-1f7c9b361ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250116} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d631a1-3d27-5d36-ade6-bd6af88af4e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ce87378-dd6f-5d41-9954-63bd96b1d88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x+6){csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250114} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bfffeab-c1bb-5a99-8bc0-97e93b39a1b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba826e94-e0a4-5375-be6a-13d761ae6472" :question/math-academy-id "q-31352" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ when $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡[(x+6)^{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u={v}^{2},\\,v=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=2v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2(x+6){csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6929b6fc-f997-55bb-b859-bdbf49ae687d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250120} {:answer/id #uuid "271ca77b-3cc4-5162-a309-7add8c31eae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250121} {:answer/id #uuid "fd73b4ef-573a-5284-bb60-652c194d7c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250122} {:answer/id #uuid "42d361d0-48da-55fe-86e7-357e129e89e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250123} {:answer/id #uuid "85da8d42-3791-5323-9e6d-f68d3ff43689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42d361d0-48da-55fe-86e7-357e129e89e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250123} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd56ad7-c1af-5c1d-ba9a-73dc89fe0447" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250119}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d323b0c1-7294-50f7-8607-7555968ab5bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-31361" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(5x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=5x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1))\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5(0)-1) \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-20(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5394d2d1-2862-54c9-bf13-ac330c8057ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250127} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf509ac-948c-502e-925a-522182506b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5394d2d1-2862-54c9-bf13-ac330c8057ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250127} :answer-field/id #uuid "af18e130-03b9-588e-ae2c-61e2f95a436b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dabb04a7-0470-529a-9e2a-3b9e2c1be042" :question/math-academy-id "q-342927" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ when $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{u},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,v=2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd2c923a-3229-5cec-8fc8-f0110187612c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(4x)+4\\cos(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd2c923a-3229-5cec-8fc8-f0110187612c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(4x)+4\\cos(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250131} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc23796-5445-50a2-9807-f38542b4f6f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250130}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a7c912f-983c-5247-a010-73ac3f66fd43" :question/math-academy-id "q-343332" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}} $){e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcf55986-23c1-53f3-baf2-54dc3e95910e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcf55986-23c1-53f3-baf2-54dc3e95910e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250134} :answer-field/id #uuid "69397bbd-19b2-59fc-8ad3-025cbb5c2afe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250133}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfa96c3e-3660-5ea3-8618-6409b518e6bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343546" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{x}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and the denominator $v(x)=x.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(1)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(-1)-0}{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efb2187f-87ee-5a67-8776-3d0d640d6acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8e^3}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efb2187f-87ee-5a67-8776-3d0d640d6acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8e^3}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250137} :answer-field/id #uuid "af76b1f0-c145-5775-8da2-ee7237e43d48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc7457da-add5-5033-834e-3210499e9469" :question/math-academy-id "q-343576" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{x+2},$ find ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\n${y}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={e}^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x+2.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{3x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{e}^{3x})(x+2)-{e}^{3x}(1)}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+6-1)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+5)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of ${y}^{′}(x)$ at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{(3(1)+5)({e}^{3(1)})}{((1)+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d72f78a-c78c-5ac0-86e9-dea6b39ff6aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250140} {:answer/id #uuid "68be3b11-51d6-57ae-8518-06a2a6b17506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250141} {:answer/id #uuid "6a92cbf4-de97-5ac8-89f6-99cdead0ac10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250142} {:answer/id #uuid "3870f6f5-4c57-5a21-b7d4-af0521206254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250143} {:answer/id #uuid "e22e46fd-ce89-5b13-b282-ba9521c17cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a92cbf4-de97-5ac8-89f6-99cdead0ac10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250142} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1e3b272-65e1-5e06-b09c-f96760f72630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250139}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef4f244d-2de2-52d7-9bcd-09d23d44c0c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-9942" :question/problem "If $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2))+({x}^{2})(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
